var LAW=[
['Habeas Corpus Explained','Latin for "you shall have the body." A court order forcing authorities to justify why someone is detained. One of the oldest safeguards against unlawful imprisonment.'],
['Offer & Acceptance','A valid contract needs offer, acceptance, consideration and intention to create legal relations. Silence is generally not acceptance.'],
['Mens Rea vs Actus Reus','Most crimes need a guilty act (actus reus) and a guilty mind (mens rea). Strict liability offences are the exception.'],
['Donoghue v Stevenson','The 1932 case behind the modern duty of care: take reasonable care to avoid harming your "neighbour".'],
['How to Write an IRAC','Issue, Rule, Application, Conclusion. Spend most of your words on the Application.'],
['Burden of Proof','Criminal cases: beyond reasonable doubt. Civil cases: balance of probabilities. The standard shapes every trial strategy.'],
['Stare Decisis','Courts follow precedent to keep the law consistent. Higher courts bind lower courts.'],
['Miranda Rights','Before custodial questioning, suspects must be told they can stay silent and have a lawyer.'],
['Contract Remedies','Damages, specific performance, rescission and restitution. Damages aim to put the claimant where they would have been.']];
